ablocks - i3bar status command with animated blocks
====================================================
A small i3blocks replacement in the suckless style: blocks are compiled in
from config.h, there is no runtime config file, and the only dependency is
libc. Block scripts written for i3blocks run unchanged.


Requirements
------------
A C99 compiler and make. i3bar to display the output.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your setup (ablocks is installed into the
/usr/local namespace by default), then:

    make clean install

The first build copies config.def.h to config.h; edit config.h and
rebuild to change the blocks.


Running ablocks
---------------
In the bar section of the i3 config:

    status_command ablocks


Blocks
------
Each block runs its command through /bin/sh on its interval (Once = at
startup only), asynchronously, with BLOCK_NAME set. Output is read the
i3blocks way:

    line 1   full_text
    line 2   short_text
    line 3   color
    @...     animation request, see below

Exit code 33 marks the block urgent. A block with empty output is hidden.


Animations
----------
An animation in config.h is a list of pango frames wrapped around a
block's output: "%s" is the output itself, "%a" an argument from the
script. Use alpha (fades without touching the script's own colours) and
rise (moves the text without changing its width), so the bar never shifts
while one plays.

A script asks for an animation by printing an extra line:

    @name [ms [arg]]

and keeps printing it for as long as the condition holds. The animation
plays `loops` times, then again every `every` seconds, and stops as soon
as the line goes. ms overrides the frame time (0 keeps the default).
ablocks sets ABLOCKS=1 for its scripts; test it before printing the line,
since i3blocks would show it as the block's short text:

    if [ -n "$ABLOCKS" ] && [ "$capacity" -le 20 ]; then
        echo "@lowbat 0 $capacity%"
    fi

Shift+scroll on such a block changes its speed live, and a notification
shows the new frame time to copy into config.h. Plain scrolling still
goes to the script.

Each time ablocks starts, i.e. on every i3 restart, bootanim plays across
the bar as a wave, one block after another from left to right; a block
with an animation requested plays it straight after its turn. Replay the
wave with:

    pkill -USR2 -x ablocks

bootdelay gives the blocks time to print before the wave starts and
bootstagger sets the gap between neighbours; keep it a multiple of the
frame time so frames change together. Set bootanim to NULL to disable.
